🌳 Tree — 
🔹 1. Root Node

Definition:
The topmost node of a tree is called the root node.

Example:

   A

👉 A is the root node

🔹 2. Parent Node

Definition:
A node that has one or more children is called a parent node.

Example:

   A
  /
 B

👉 A is parent of B

🔹 3. Child Node

Definition:
A node directly connected below a parent node is called a child node.

Example:

   A
  /
 B

👉 B is child of A

🔹 4. Sibling Nodes

Definition:
Nodes that have the same parent are called sibling nodes.

Example:

   A
  / \
 B   C

👉 B and C are siblings

🔹 5. Edge

Definition:
A connection between two nodes is called an edge.

Example:

A — B

👉 Property:
A tree with n nodes has (n − 1) edges

🔹 6. Degree of a Node

Definition:
The number of children of a node is called its degree.

Example:

     A
   / | \
  B  C  D

👉 Degree of A = 3

🔹 7. Degree of a Tree

Definition:
The maximum degree of any node in the tree is called the degree of the tree.

🔹 8. Internal Node

Definition:
A node that has at least one child is called an internal node.

Example:

     A
    / \
   B   C
  /
 D

👉 A and B are internal nodes

🔹 9. Leaf Node

Definition:
A node that has no children is called a leaf node.

Example:

     A
    / \
   B   C
  /
 D

👉 C and D are leaf nodes

🔹 10. Level

Definition:
The position of a node in a tree is called its level.
Root is at level 0.

Example:

Level 0:   A
          / \
Level 1: B   C
        /
Level 2:D
🔹 11. Height of Node

Definition:
The number of edges in the longest path from that node to a leaf node.

🔹 12. Height of a Tree

Definition:
The number of edges in the longest path from the root to a leaf node.

Example:

     A
    / \
   B   C
  /
 D

👉 Height = 2

🔹 13. Depth

Definition:
The number of edges from the root node to a given node.

👉 Example: Depth of D = 2

🔹 14. Subtree

Definition:
A subtree is a tree formed by a node and all of its descendants.

Example:

     A
    / \
   B   C
  /
 D

Subtree of B:

   B
  /
 D
🌳 Types of Trees (Exam Format)
🔹 1. General Tree

Definition:
A tree in which a node can have any number of children.

Example:

      A
    / | \
   B  C  D
      |
      E
🔹 2. Binary Tree

Definition:
A tree in which each node has at most two children.

Example:

     A
    / \
   B   C
🔹 3. Binary Search Tree (BST)

Definition:
A binary tree in which:

Left subtree nodes are less than root
Right subtree nodes are greater than root

Example:

      10
     /  \
    5   20


